          "rn.modal.p1_title": "What is Retinopathy of Prematurity?",
          "rn.modal.p1_text": "ROP (Retinopathy of Prematurity) is a severe eye disorder in premature infants caused by an avascular (bloodless) zone of the retina and abnormal vessel growth (fibrovascular proliferation), which can lead to retinal detachment and permanent visual disability.",
          "rn.modal.p2_title": "Retinal Vascular Timeline",
          "rn.modal.p2_text": "Normally in a fetus, retinal blood vessels begin to develop from 12–16 weeks of gestation, continuing until 38–40 weeks.",
          "rn.modal.p3_title": "Pathology Mechanism",
          "rn.modal.p3_text": "Disruption of normal vessel development leads to tortuosity and formation of abnormal new vessels at the demarcation line between vascular and avascular retina. This causes fibrovascular tissue growth toward the anterior segment, resulting in retinal traction and detachment.",
          "rn.modal.p4_title": "Critical Observation Window & Screening",
          "rn.modal.p4_text": "ROP is one of the most common complications in premature babies. It primarily develops between 5–15 weeks after birth, during which expert pediatric eye examination is mandatory.",
          "rn.modal.p5_title": "Stages & Consequences",
          "rn.modal.p5_text": "The disease progresses through 5 stages. Without timely treatment, it can result in lifelong irreversible blindness. Screening continues for 4–5 months.",
